WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.981
The checklist.

2
00:00:01.261 --> 00:00:05.933
Split the suite into capability and regression evals, and gate on regression.

3
00:00:06.213 --> 00:00:19.675
Tier checks by cost: deterministic tests and replays on every commit, a live smoke set on PRs that change model behavior, the full uncached suite on a schedule, a human sample before releases.

4
00:00:19.955 --> 00:00:28.275
Gate on must-pass cases and a paired lower bound with a margin you fixed in advance, and size the suite so identical systems pass.

5
00:00:28.555 --> 00:00:30.176
Never rerun until green.

6
00:00:30.456 --> 00:00:37.667
Key cached responses on everything that changes the output, pin model ids, and run something uncached regularly.

7
00:00:37.947 --> 00:00:44.091
Keep prompts, datasets, graders, and results in your repository with a manifest for every run.

8
00:00:44.371 --> 00:00:50.941
Lesson 9 uses all of this to answer the question the industry argues about most: which model should we use?

9
00:00:51.221 --> 00:00:52.523
A team note to finish.

10
00:00:52.803 --> 00:00:56.323
Give the suite an owner, the way a service has an on-call owner.

11
00:00:56.603 --> 00:01:07.632
Someone has to triage a red nightly run, decide whether a failing case is a real regression or a stale reference answer, and retire cases that no longer earn their cost.

12
00:01:07.912 --> 00:01:12.179
Without an owner, an eval suite decays into a dashboard nobody reads.
